e branch and reads `review-feedback.md`. When the fix lands with code changes, every requested reviewer runs again at the new head (their `:done` and `:changes` labels come off), because merge-gating approvals must cover the merged head. A round that changes no code, or one that would pass `max_review_rounds` (default 3), posts one comment and adds `ard:needs-human` on the issue; it is not repeated on the same head after a person removes the label. Removing `ard:needs-human` and pushing a new head caps again at once and never reruns reviewers; to continue, raise max_review_rounds, or fix the PR and remove the ard:review:<role>:changes labels so reviewers rerun. Merger conflict and rebase rounds are a follow-up that will use the same round API (`reviewfix.RequestRound`); until then a conflict, like a red required check, goes to `ard:needs-human`.\n+**Review feedback loops back here.** The `reviewfix` loop (#41) starts a round when every requested reviewer has finished and at least one ended `ard:review:<role>:changes`, backed by that role's close-out record (a label alone does not count). It records a `review_round` event in `_controller.db` (counted when requested, ordered by event ID, local to that database) and removes `ard:worker:<role>:done` from the issue, so the worker loop claims it again. The worker fixes on the same branch and reads `review-feedback.md`. When the fix lands with code changes, every requested reviewer runs again at the new head (their `:done` and `:changes` labels come off), because merge-gating approvals must cover the merged head. A round that changes no code, or one that would pass `max_review_rounds` (default 3), posts one comment and adds `ard:needs-human` on the issue; it is not repeated on the same head after a person removes the label. Removing `ard:needs-human` and pushing a new head caps again at once and never reruns reviewers; to continue, raise max_review_rounds, or fix the PR and remove the ard:review:<role>:changes labels so re